Friedrich wrote:

 > function vision_recalc contains a bug that leaves
 > mobile light source at one place when becoming
 > blind.

I've always assumed that was intentionally the way
the code behaved.

It is consistent with other similar situations in
NetHack for cases where the PC is blind or out of
line of sight of some change.

* Corpses earlier seen by the PC but evaporating
   while the PC cannot see them remain displayed
   until the PC sees them gone or feels them gone.

* Objects once seen by the PC but no longer seen
   remain displayed even if moved by monsters, until
   the PC sees them gone or feels them gone.

     This case is especially intriguing when the PC
     picks up a uniquely named object or artifact,
     that still displays on its former location
     despite that the PC _knows_ it cannot possibly
     still be there.

* Walls that the PC has seen, but that are removed
   by digging monsters while the PC cannot see them,
   remain displayed as intact until the PC can see
   them again.

* Any of the "detect foo" scrolls/potions, when
   used, put stuff on the map that may or may not
   still be there when the PC arrives in line of
   sight of the location, but are remembered as
   being at the displayed locations up until then.

It helps to conceptualize the level map as a map of
what the PC _remembers_, not necessarily what the
current reality _is_.

If the PC _is not_ blind, everything in line of
sight updates in real time, but nothing out of line
of sight does, because the PC remembers them as last
detected.

If the PC _is_ blind, not even things in line of
sight update in real time, only things discovered by
searching, by bumping into them, or by stepping on
them.

Lighted area are an even more constrained case for a
map that shows what the PC _remembers_.

Since lighted areas cannot be searched for, nor
felt, nor bumped into, the PC should have to be
_seeing_ lighting changes for them to be updated in
real time. [Does that work right for scrolls of
light when the PC is blind? I know it does for the
"out of line of sight" case.]

What the level map display contains is what the PC
_remembers_ seeing when last able to see that
location, not what would be seen now if the PC were
not blind or were not out of line of sight of the
location.

So if the PC remembers some spot as being lighted,
that's what the just what the map keeps showing
until better information is available to the PC.

Nor can spots newly lighted be detected while blind
or out of line of sight.

[In particular, if a scroll of light is read such
that grid locations behind a visual barrier become
lighted, the PC doesn't see them as having become
lighted until moving to bring them into line of
sight.]

